window.docContents[165].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Roots-of-unity-average___-either-all-equal-or-sum-is-zero/#Chapter5___Lemma5___4___5":{"contents":"Lemma 5.4.5. If \\varepsilon_1, \\varepsilon_2, \\ldots, \\varepsilon_n are roots of unity such that \\frac{1}{n}(\\varepsilon_1 + \\varepsilon_2 + \\cdots + \\varepsilon_n) is an algebraic integer, then either \\varepsilon_1 = \\cdots = \\varepsilon_n or \\varepsilon_1 + \\cdots + \\varepsilon_n = 0.\nProof. Let a = \\frac{1}{n}(\\varepsilon_1 + \\cdots + \\varepsilon_n). If not all \\varepsilon_i are equal, then |a| < 1. Moreover, since any algebraic conjugate of a root of unity is also a root of unity, |a'| \\leq 1 for any algebraic conjugate a' of a. But the product of all algebraic conjugates of a is an integer. Since it has absolute value < 1, it must equal zero. Therefore, a = 0. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.4. Burnside's theorem\tRoots of unity average: either all equal or sum is zero","header":"Roots of unity average: either all equal or sum is zero","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Roots-of-unity-average___-either-all-equal-or-sum-is-zero/#Chapter5___Lemma5___4___5"}});
